---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import DnsHunt from '../components/DnsHunt.astro';
import { withBase } from '../config/site';
---

<Base
  title="DNS hunt"
  description="Simulated DNS threat hunt on synthetic Pi-hole data. Use hunting leads, investigate each name, judge it, compare with the notes."
  wide
>
  <p class="path">./dns-hunt --simulated</p>
  <h1>DNS hunt</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    One day of DNS lookups from a made-up home network, as a filter like Pi-hole would log them. Every device asks DNS
    before it talks to anything, so these names show what each device contacted. Most are routine. A few are not.
  </p>

  <div class="sim-banner" role="note">
    <strong>Simulated environment</strong>
    <span>
      Every name ends in .example or .test (reserved so they can never be real sites) and every address comes from
      ranges reserved for documentation. The scenario is modeled on hunting my own lab's Pi-hole logs; none of the data
      is from it.
    </span>
  </div>

  <h2 class="how-title">How to hunt</h2>
  <ol class="steps prose">
    <li>Turn on a <strong>lead</strong>, such as "Random-looking part". Hunters start with simple rules like these.</li>
    <li>Open a name. Read the device, how often it was looked up, the answer and any timing note.</li>
    <li>
      Judge it: <strong>Normal</strong>, <strong>Telemetry or ads</strong> (unwanted, not an attack), or
      <strong>Suspicious</strong> (needs investigation). Then read why.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p class="prose muted">
    Each lead also catches harmless names. Notice which ones, and look for devices where several signs line up.
  </p>

  <DnsHunt />

  <details class="how">
    <summary>What the measurements mean</summary>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        <strong>Length and parts.</strong> Long names with many dot-separated parts can carry hidden data, but cloud
        services and Windows use them too.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Randomness</strong> is Shannon entropy of the longest part, in bits per character. Ordinary words land
        around 2.5 to 3; machine-generated strings climb above 3.4. Server IDs in content networks are random too, so
        check the parent name.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>NXDOMAIN</strong> means the name doesn't exist. One failure is a typo; dozens of different random names
        failing from one device is a pattern.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Per day.</strong> Exact, round-the-clock regularity suggests a program on a timer. Telemetry does this
        too; what matters is which device, to whom, and whether the name is honest about it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The data and the scoring logic are described on the <a href={withBase('/projects/dns-hunt/')}>project page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
</Base>

<style>
  .how-title {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    margin: 1.75rem 0 0.5rem;
  }
  .steps {
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
  }
  .steps li {
    margin: 0.3rem 0;
  }
  .how {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding-top: 1rem;
  }
  .how summary {
    cursor: pointer;
    font-weight: 600;
    padding: 0.5rem 0;
  }
</style>
